          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0
    ,
      "why": "Full pipeline gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput = Fmax(MHz) >= 1 MSPS is easily met for any reasonable Fmax. Area is dominated by the N registered stages; keeping N in 10..16 and W in 12..16 keeps luts_plus_ffs low while accuracy_bits (driven by W, N, guards) stays >= 10. This family has the cleanest latency/throughput trade-off for the >=1 MSPS constraint."
    }
  ,
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "param": "data_width",
          "low": 12,
          "high": 16
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "n_iter",
          "low": 10,
          "high": 16
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "angle_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "frac_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        },
        {
          "param": "m",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 4
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0
    ,
      "why": "Registering every m stages cuts FF count versus full pipelined while still yielding 1 result/cycle (throughput = Fmax >= 1 MSPS). m in 2..4 trades a small latency increase for meaningful FF savings, which directly serves the min luts_plus_ffs objective. Same W/N/guard ranges as pipelined to keep accuracy_bits >= 10 and area low."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Both allowed families produce 1 result/cycle, so the throughput_msps >= 1 constraint reduces to Fmax >= 1 MHz, which is trivially satisfied; the real trade-off is area (luts_plus_ffs) vs accuracy_bits. Accuracy depends only on W, N, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding, so I bound W in 12..16 and N in 10..16: W=12 with N~12-14 and small guards should already push max_abs_err below 2^-10 (accuracy_bits >= 10), while W=16/N=16 gives headroom for the accuracy objective. Guards are kept small (0..2) because area is the primary objective and extra guard bits add LUT/FF cost with diminishing accuracy returns. pipelined_m with m=2..4 is included because it can beat full pipelined on FF count at the same throughput, potentially dominating on the luts_plus_ffs axis. Splitting budget evenly lets NSGA-II map the Pareto front across both families and find where pipelined_m's FF savings cross over pipelined's lower latency."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The spec is satisfied with a strong margin on the critical constraint. The Pareto front contains 5 feasible designs, all meeting throughput >= 1 MSPS (best 97.8 MSPS, well above 1) and max_abs_err <= 2^-10 (best 0.000177, which is 2^-12.47, significantly better than the 0.000976 limit). The primary objective, minimizing luts_plus_ffs, is well-covered with the best design at 995 LUTs+FFs, which is comfortably below the HV reference of 1500. The accuracy objective is also well-covered, ranging from 10.7 to 12.5 bits, exceeding the HV reference of 10. The front spans a reasonable range of the area objective (995-1226) and accuracy (10.7-12.5). Since this is the final round, and the front already provides a clear, feasible, and high-quality trade-off curve with the minimum area design at 995 LUTs+FFs, further exploration is unnecessary. The selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) will pick the 995 LUTs+FFs design, which is robust and feasible."
,
  "next_plan": null
}
